From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Subject: [PATCH] mount-util: always switch root with MS_MOVE

mount_switch_root_full() tucks the new root under the old one with
pivot_root(".", ".") and then runs umount2(".", MNT_DETACH), relying on that to
act on the top-most mount at the cwd, the old root.

On the LuneOS Linux 3.4 Halium machines it does not. umount2(".") resolves to
the mount the cwd itself is on, so it detaches the new root and leaves the
process in a mount that belongs to no namespace. The next mount() on it faults
in the kernel:

  mount(NULL, "/", NULL, MS_REC|MS_SHARED, NULL) -> killed by SIGSEGV
  Unable to handle kernel NULL pointer dereference at virtual address 00000014
  PC is at do_mount+0x52c/0xb24

Every unit that sets up a mount namespace (ProtectSystem=, PrivateTmp=, ...)
dies this way, including systemd-udevd, systemd-logind and systemd-userdbd.

Reproduced outside systemd, in a forked child on a booted device: setting the
propagation of "." works right after pivot_root() and crashes only after
umount2(".", MNT_DETACH). The MS_MOVE and chroot() sequence the function
already has as its fallback does the switch without crashing, and a following
propagation change succeeds.

Set force_ms_move so the fallback is always taken.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [systemd's minimum kernel is much newer than 3.4]
---
--- a/src/shared/mount-util.c
+++ b/src/shared/mount-util.c
@@ -517,6 +517,12 @@
         if (fchdir(fd_newroot) < 0)
                 return log_debug_errno(errno, "Failed to chdir into new rootfs '%s': %m", path);
 
+        /* mount_switch_root_pivot() relies on umount2(".", MNT_DETACH) acting on the top-most mount at the
+         * cwd. A 3.4 kernel resolves "." to the mount the cwd itself is on, so it detaches the new root
+         * instead of the stacked old one, and the next mount() on it dereferences a NULL mount namespace
+         * and oopses. MS_MOVE plus chroot() behaves the same on every kernel, so always use it. */
+        force_ms_move = true;
+
         /* Make this a NOP if we are supposed to switch to our current root fs. After all, both pivot_root()
          * and MS_MOVE don't like that. */
         if (!is_current_root) {
